    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "In the 1800s locals called the forested area of South Venice “the pinewoods” and tried various short‑lived farms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://southvenice.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1920s saw a railroad from Nocatee Freight Company to the sawmill town of Woodmere, linking the area to regional trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://southvenice.org/history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1952 two New Jersey brothers bought 3,000 acres and plotted 19,587 lots, founding the South Venice Subdivision.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://southvenice.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1956 developers dedicated 1,600 feet of Gulf Beach to residents, adding bridges that later vanished when the Intracoastal Waterway was dredged.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://southvenice.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Corps compensated the loss by buying a ferry and building docks, restoring beach access after the 1965 waterway construction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://southvenice.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "South Venice sits west of North Port, south of Venice, and north of Englewood, placing it in the heart of the Sarasota‑Sarasota‑Venice metro area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
